        <mxCell id="e405" value="is counted on" style="edgeStyle=entityRelationEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;fontSize=10;startArrow=ERone;startFill=0;endArrow=ERmany;endFill=0;strokeColor=#4a5468;fontColor=#4a5468;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="material" target="stock_count_line"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e406" value="counts" style="edgeStyle=entityRelationEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;fontSize=10;startArrow=ERone;startFill=0;endArrow=ERmany;endFill=0;strokeColor=#4a5468;fontColor=#4a5468;exitX=0;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=1;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="stock_count" target="stock_count_line"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e407" value="is put right by" style="edgeStyle=entityRelationEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;fontSize=10;startArrow=ERone;startFill=0;endArrow=ERmany;endFill=0;strokeColor=#4a5468;fontColor=#4a5468;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="stock_count" target="stock_adjustment"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n4" value="A stock_receipt is what was bought on one receipt or supplier&apos;s invoice: from whom, on which day, its own number, who paid -- a person, who is owed it back, or the business when empty -- the tax it charged, all of it, and the paper itself, kept once. Each item on it is a lot, in its own unit, carrying its share of that tax, split by what each cost before tax.

A material_lot is stock on the shelf: what all of it cost before tax and its share of the tax, as the receipt says them, so a unit&apos;s share is worked out from those and nothing is lost to rounding a cost per foot. It came on a receipt or was found, one and never both, and the oldest is drawn first.

A line drawn from stock takes off the oldest lots first (stock_draw), and an adjustment taken off does the same (stock_take). A trigger keeps qty_remaining following both, so taking more than is left is refused by the lot&apos;s own check.

A stock_adjustment puts one material&apos;s stock right by hand: so much off the shelf or on it, on a day, and why. Used, damaged, lost and returned only take off; found only puts on, as a lot of its own; a correction or a count goes either way. What it moved is held at what it cost.

A stock_count is what was on the shelf on a day: open while it is counted, one open at a time, and applied once, when each difference becomes an adjustment that names it. Each line keeps what the shelf said when it was counted beside what was there, so stock that moved meanwhile is told rather than written over, and a cost each only where the shelf has nothing to cost what turned up by.

A receipt, an adjustment and a count keep the client_uuid the page or the phone made them with: sent twice, each is one." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;fontColor=#7b8698;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="760" width="1540" height="330" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
